{"attack": true,"case": "The claim's stated object of preference is 'better-corroborated theories,' but the mechanism silently substitutes a different selection criterion: 'the one with the highest empirical content (boldness).' In Popper's framework these are distinct: corroboration is backward-looking (a theory has survived severe past tests), while empirical content/boldness is forward-looking (a theory makes risky, testable predictions). A bold new theory can have maximal empirical content yet zero corroboration; a highly corroborated theory may have already exhausted its novel testable content. The mechanism therefore does not justify the preference that the claim actually asserts — it justifies a preference for the boldest untested theory, which is a different and stronger claim than Popper's preference for the better-corroborated one. This internal inconsistency means the defense does not establish what it claims to establish. Additionally, the mechanism's closing assertion — 'Unlike every previously studied system of this kind, which faced identical structural constraints yet exhibited none of this behaviour' — is a sweeping universal empirical claim made with empty premises, empty derivation, no formalization refs, and no simulation refs, rendering it wholly unsupported.","counterexample": [{"description": "Theory A: highly corroborated (survived 100 severe tests), moderate remaining empirical content. Theory B: maximal empirical content (bold novel predictions), zero corroboration (untested). The mechanism selects B (highest empirical content for future falsification), but the claim says the preference is for 'better-corroborated theories' — which would select A. The mechanism and the claim yield opposite preferences."}],"premise": "In Popper's framework, corroboration (past survival of severe tests) and empirical content/boldness (forward-looking testability) are distinct properties; the mechanism selects on the latter while the claim asserts a preference